    {
      "id": "library/10665-59577/blocks/prose-page-006",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Page 6",
      "pageStart": 6,
      "pageEnd": 6,
      "target": "../sections/page-006.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO/CDS/AIDS/86.1\npage 4\n\nWorldwide, the most important wode of transmission is sexual,\nLAV/RTLV-ILL can spread bi-directionally between heterosexual or homosexual\npartners, Transmission of LAV/ATLV-III through semen donation has also been\ndocumented, Parenteral LAV/HTLV-IIL transmission can occur through\ntransfusion of whole blood, blood cells, platelets, or factors VIII or IX\nderived from contaminated human plasma, In addition, the accidental or\nintentional sharing or reuse of contamined (unsterilized) needles, syringes,\nscalpels, razors, or other skin or mucous membrane-piereing inetruments",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Explains that sexual transmission is the most important route worldwide, alongside transmission through blood, blood products, shared needles and from mother to child, and that the virus is not spread by casual contact, insects, food or water. It begins describing clinical features and laboratory methods for detecting infection.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "library/10665-59577/blocks/prose-page-007",
