      "pageEnd": 7,
      "target": "../sections/page-007.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO/CDS/AIDS/86.1\npage 5\n\ninfection in the community, assessing the likelihood that an individual has\nbeen exposed to LAV/HILV-III, and evaluating various methods of preventing or\ntreating the disease,\n\nAt present, the only practical approach for routine or large-scale testing\nis detection of antibody to LAV/HTLV-III and the enzyme-linked immunosorbent\nassay (ELISA) is the antibody detection method most frequently used, ELISA\ntest systems are highly sensitive (98%); however, not all reactive sera are\nindicative of LAV/HTLV-III infections because of non-specific reactions, In\ngeneral, the perce",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes antibody testing by ELISA, which is highly sensitive but gives some false positives, so repeatedly reactive samples should be confirmed with another test. It then introduces the WHO/CDC case definition of AIDS based on opportunistic diseases indicating cellular immunodeficiency.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
