      "pageEnd": 12,
      "target": "../sections/page-012.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "WHO/CDS/AIDS/86,1\npage 10\n\nblood or other body fluids should be used once and disposed or be thoroughly\ncleaned and disinfected (see section 7,1).\n\n2.4 Management of parenteral and mucous membrane exposure\n\nlt a HCW has a parenteral (e.g,, needlestick or cut) or mucous membrane\n(e.g., splash to eye or mouth) exposure to blood or other body fluids, the\nsource patient should be assessed clinically and epidemiologically to\ndetermine the likelihood of LAV/HTLV-1II infection. In some situations\nserologic testing of the source patient may be considered. If the source\npatient has evidence of LAV/HTLV",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes managing needlestick or mucous membrane exposures, including assessing the source patient and testing and following up exposed workers for up to a year. It then sets out laboratory precautions, such as using biological safety cabinets for procedures likely to create aerosols.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
